1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
On most projects that call Clod, the integration code hits a pull request and it needs the same

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:11,000
review as anything else. This lesson covers what to look for, how to review error handling, retries

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:17,000
and response validation, and how Clod itself can assist while a person still sits in the pipeline

4
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:25,000
and gives final approval. Let's dig in. Let's start with the three focus areas that matter most

5
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:32,000
when you review Clod integration code. First, error handling. Good code classifies each failure

6
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:38,000
by type before it reacts, then retries transient errors and fixes or escalates permanent ones.

7
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Second, response validation. The code should check the model response against a schema or

8
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:50,000
business rules after generation and re-prompt with the error on failure so the model can correct

9
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:57,000
itself. Third, defensive parsing. The code consumes output while anticipating malformed

10
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:04,000
or incomplete content so a single bad generation never crashes the pipeline. Error handling,

11
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:10,000
response validation, defensive parsing. Keep those three in view and most review problems surface

12
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:17,000
quickly. Zoom in on error handling because it's where integration code quietly goes wrong. The

13
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:23,000
first thing I look for is whether the code classifies the error before it reacts. A timeout

14
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:29,000
isn't a bad request and treating them the same is the root of a lot of flaky behavior. The classic

15
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:35,000
smell is a single catch-all block that treats every exception identically and retries it. That

16
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:42,000
looks robust at a glance but it isn't. Once the type is known, the response should match it. Back

17
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:49,000
off and retry on one side. Fix, fall back, or escalate on the other. A retry loop wrapped

18
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:55,000
around a bad request just burns budget and never succeeds because a permanent failure returns the

19
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:02,000
same answer on every attempt. When you see that pattern in a review, flag it and ask the author

20
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:08,000
to branch on the error type first. You know the transient versus permanent split cold by now

21
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:15,000
so in review it compresses into one question. Is the code only retrying the errors that can

22
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:21,000
actually clear? Timeouts, rate limits, and 500 series blips clear so back off and retry around

23
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:29,000
those is exactly right. A bad request, a malformed schema, or an auth failure returns the same result

24
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:36,000
every time so a retry wrapped around one is a defect hiding the real problem and burning budget

25
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:42,000
while it does. Picture a nightly job retrying a malformed request three times per record across

26
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:49,000
a huge data set. Every attempt fails the same way and the log fills with noise instead of one clear

27
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:55,000
error you could have fixed in minutes. Ask that one question of every retry policy you review

28
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:02,000
and the classification does the rest of the work for you. Now response validation which is where

29
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:07,000
confident but wrong output gets caught. Claude states a wrong answer with the same fluency as a

30
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:13,000
right one so review can't stop at whether the call returned successfully. I want to see the response

31
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:20,000
checked against a schema or against business rules after it comes back and on failure the code should

32
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:26,000
reprompt with the specific error so the model gets a chance to correct itself. The full loop is

33
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:34,000
generate, validate, and reprompt not a one-shot check. Watch for validation that only checks syntax

34
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:40,000
or that only runs at the very end. Semantic checks plus a correction path are what actually catch a

35
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:46,000
plausible but wrong result. Defensive parsing is the habit of consuming model output as if it might

36
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:53,000
surprise you because sometimes it will. Three checks show up when I review it. First, tolerant extraction.

37
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:58,000
The code reads fields without assuming they're present or well typed and it handles a missing or

38
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:05,000
null value on purpose. Second, explicit failure. When the output doesn't match what the parser expected

39
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:13,000
it fails safely and loudly rather than proceeding on half-formed data. Third, no blind trust. Even with

40
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:18,000
structured outputs turned on downstream code shouldn't treat a confident looking response

41
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:23,000
as a guaranteed contract. Treating a fluent response as certain is exactly the mistake

42
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:30,000
defensive parsing exists to prevent. As we established prompts are behavior so prompt edits

43
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:36,000
and schema edits deserve the same scrutiny as application logic not a rubber stamp. When a

44
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:42,000
prompt changes I want the diff to show exactly what shifted because a small wording tweak can move the

45
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:48,000
model's output in ways a reviewer needs to see. When a schema changes check it against the structured

46
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:55,000
output rules your integration relies on so an edit doesn't quietly reject valid responses or

47
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:01,000
break a downstream parser. The trap is treating prompts and schemas as configuration that lives

48
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:07,000
outside code review. Their behavior, diff them, reason about the effect, and review them with the

49
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:14,000
same care you'd give a function. Readability sounds soft but it's a real review criterion

50
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:20,000
especially for code that talks to a fallible model. Clear naming comes first. A name should say

51
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:27,000
what a value or a function actually does so a reviewer can follow the flow without tracing

52
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:35,000
every call by hand. Next, readable structure. The request, the call, the validation, and the parse

53
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:41,000
should read top to bottom not hide inside one dense block that does everything at once and keep

54
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:47,000
changes reviewable in size. A small focused change is easier to reason about and approve

55
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:53,000
than a sprawling edit that mixes many concerns. If a reviewer can't hold the change in their head

56
00:05:53,000 --> 00:06:00,000
it's probably too big. Let's talk about using Clod itself as a review assistant because it's

57
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:07,000
genuinely useful here with one firm boundary. Clod can read a diff and flag likely defects,

58
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:13,000
point out missing error handling, or summarize what a prompt or schema change actually does

59
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:19,000
in plain language. That widens your review coverage and it catches things a tired human

60
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:27,000
skims past but the approval stays human. A person reviews Clod's suggestions and approves every

61
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:33,000
change before it merges. The failure mode is letting the assistant's confidence stand in for

62
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:40,000
judgment. Remember, a fluent explanation isn't proof the change is correct. Use Clod to surface

63
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:47,000
more, to explain more, and to review faster but keep a human in the approval loop. Reviewing

64
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:55,000
Clod integration code protects reliability. Classify errors before you retry. Validate responses and

65
00:06:55,000 --> 00:07:02,000
re-prompt on failure. Parse output defensively and review prompt and schema diffs like real logic.

66
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:06,000
Keep in mind a confident response is not a correct response.

67
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:11,000
That covers reviewing Clod integration code. I'll see you in the next one.

